      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1818 the town square was set aside for a courthouse that was never built, a reminder of early civic plans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofcourtland.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Just south of the square ran the Tuscumbia, Courtland and Decatur Railroad, later incorporated into the Memphis & Charleston Line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofcourtland.org/history/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Federal‑era buildings in North Courtland echo Virginia and upper‑South traditions, showcasing the town’s early architectural influences.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofcourtland.org/history/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "North Courtland offers a rare glimpse of nearly 175 years of architectural evolution, from log dogtrot cottages to Greek‑Revival homes.",
